  "question": "The electric field at a point on the axis of a short dipole is $E$. At a point at the same distance on its equatorial line, the field is",
  "diagram": "",
  "diagram_alt": "",
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$2E$, in the same direction",
      "image": ""
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\dfrac{E}{2}$, in the same direction",
      "image": ""
    },
    {
      "text": "$E$, in the opposite direction",
      "image": ""
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\dfrac{E}{2}$, in the opposite direction",
      "image": ""
    }
  ],
  "answer": "D",
  "numerical_answer": "",
  "tolerance": "",
  "solution": "Axial: $\\dfrac{2kp}{r^3}$ along $\\vec{p}$. Equatorial: $\\dfrac{kp}{r^3}$ opposite to $\\vec{p}$. So the equatorial field is $\\dfrac{E}{2}$, pointing the other way.",
